Track 3: Desfase de la Selva
- Story & Context: Months after authorities shuttered the research camp, rangers entering the Chocó Biosphere Reserve discovered that the root networks of several ancient strangler figs had entirely engulfed the abandoned subterranean subwoofers. Fueled by rotting batteries and continuous sap currents, the trees were autonomously broadcasting low-frequency seismic hums that vibrated the morning fog at four-second intervals. Locals gathered at the perimeter to listen to ghost-like Mellotron harmonics drifting down from the high branches, convinced they heard Marcela’s disembodied voice echoing through the damp moss, "the rings of Saturn taste like rain down here." The forest had permanently internalized the machine language, creating an immortal botanical folklore that outlived the botanists themselves.
